<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apadana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 515–465 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apadana is the great audience hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> around 515 BCE and completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>. Its roof rested on seventy-two stone columns about 20 metres high. The reliefs of its staircases show delegations from the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, among them Saka in pointed caps, bringing gifts to the king, and are a key source for the appearance of these peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apadana.jpg" alt="Apadana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persepolis001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
